  "title": "Trauma and Maladaptive Plasticity",
  "emoji": "🔄",
  "color": "#7E22CE",
  "description": "Learn Trauma and Maladaptive Plasticity in one minute.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A car backfires on a quiet street and a combat veteran drops to the ground, heart pounding, fully convinced they are under fire.",
      "visual": "💥"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Trauma rewires the brain just like positive experiences do. Repeated threat exposure strengthens fear circuits in the amygdala.",
      "visual": "😰"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "In PTSD, the amygdala becomes hyperactive while the prefrontal cortex, which normally regulates fear, becomes underactive.",
      "visual": "📉"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "This is maladaptive plasticity: the brain rewired itself to survive a dangerous environment, but it cannot switch back when.",
      "visual": "🔒"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Therapies like EMDR and prolonged exposure work by promoting new plasticity, gradually rewiring fear circuits to respond normally.",
      "visual": "🔓"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Plasticity created the problem. Plasticity is also the way out.",
      "visual": "🚪"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is maladaptive plasticity in PTSD?",
    "options": [
      "The brain rewires fear circuits in ways that persist after danger passes",
      "The brain fails to form any new connections after trauma",
      "Trauma causes all neurons to die in the amygdala"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
